title: No duplicate rows
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: reference
summary: A metarecord never holds the same (name, value) twice; writes collapse duplicates instead of failing, except an edit by row id.

A metarecord's fields are a ''set'' of `(name, value)` pairs, not a bag: several
`tag` rows are allowed because their values differ, but the same tag twice says
nothing the first row does not.

Every write enforces it by ''collapsing'', never by failing:

* ''add'' of a value the metarecord already holds under that name is a no-op: no row, no new version, nothing logged. The set form's `updated` count counts only the metarecords that gained a row — re-running a tagging prints `0`.
* ''set'' (one field, or a whole metarecord) keeps the first occurrence of each value and drops the later ones.
* a ''retype'' that makes two rows equal keeps one ([[Changing a field's type]]).

The one exception is the edit ''by row id'' (`PATCH /fields/:id`, `mf field set <id>`):
it names one row, so turning it into the twin of a sibling is refused with `400`
rather than silently deleting the row the client addressed.

`nothing` is a value like any other here: a field holds at most one `nothing` row.

Log navigation is exempt: a rollback or a revert restores the exact rows a snapshot
recorded, ids included — a duplicate written before the rule comes back with it.
